Interface:

```lean
/-- Interface for the classical maximum principle for the heat equation on a space-time
slab `Icc a b × Icc 0 T`.  mathlib (pinned revision) has no heat-equation theory, so this
is a definition of the statement, with every hypothesis explicit.  It is *not* an axiom:
the structure must be constructed for a concrete solution before its `bound` field can be
used. -/
structure HeatSlabMaximumPrincipleInterface (u : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (a b T : ℝ) : Prop where
  /-- `u` satisfies the heat equation `∂ₜ u = ∂ₓ² u` in the interior of the slab. -/
  heat_equation : ∀ x t, x ∈ Icc a b → t ∈ Ioo 0 T →
    deriv (fun s => u x s) t = iteratedDeriv 2 (fun y => u y t) x
  /-- `u` is continuous on the closed slab. -/
  continuousOn_slab : ContinuousOn (fun p : ℝ × ℝ => u p.1 p.2) (Icc a b ×ˢ Icc 0 T)
  /-- The initial datum is nonpositive. -/
  initial_nonpos : ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, u x 0 ≤ 0
  /-- The lateral boundary data are nonpositive. -/
  lateral_nonpos : ∀ t ∈ Icc 0 T, u a t ≤ 0 ∧ u b t ≤ 0
  /-- Conclusion of the parabolic maximum principle: `u ≤ 0` on the slab. -/
  bound_nonpos : ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, ∀ t ∈ Icc 0 T, u x t ≤ 0
```

And a checked lemma:
```lean
/-- Using the interface: any function carrying the heat-slab maximum-principle interface is
nonpositive on the slab.  (Checked projection lemma showing the interface is usable.) -/
theorem heat_slab_nonpos_of_interface {u : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ} {a b T : ℝ}
    (h : HeatSlabMaximumPrincipleInterface u a b T) :
    ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, ∀ t ∈ Icc 0 T, u x t ≤ 0 :=
  h.bound_nonpos
```

